<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/advanced/comparative-politics-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Comparative politics wants the answers only experiments give and the subject matter forbids:
    you cannot randomise constitutions, assign revolutions, or rerun decolonisation. The field's
    response is a rigorous craft of observational design — treating the world's roughly 200
    countries as cases whose similarities and differences can be arranged to do the work of
    control. This chapter is that craft: the two classic comparison designs, the disciplined
    use of case studies, process tracing as causal detective work, and the honest reckoning with
    small n and non-independence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the two classic designs, the case-study defence, and the small-n reality" widget="equationStep" config={compCfg} />

  <h2>Mill's two designs, at country scale</h2>
  <p>
    Mill's <strong>method of difference</strong> becomes the <strong>most similar systems design
    (MSSD)</strong>: find countries alike on every plausibly relevant dimension — colonial
    history, resource endowment, religion, income — differing on the suspected cause (electoral
    rule, party system, colonial administrative style), and differing correspondingly on the
    outcome. The shared factors act as controls by construction; the one difference carries the
    suspicion. <strong>Worked example (Duverger's law):</strong> the UK and the Netherlands are
    both wealthy European parliamentary democracies; the UK's first-past-the-post elections run
    with a stable two-party system, the Netherlands' proportional representation with a fragmented
    multiparty one — the electoral formula is the standing suspect for party-system shape, and
    the mechanism (wasted votes under FPTP push supporters toward viable challengers) is
    testable in constituencies. Mill's <strong>method of agreement</strong> becomes the
    <strong>most different systems design (MDSD)</strong>: countries unalike in almost everything
    that share the same outcome — say, durable democracy in Costa Rica, Mauritius and Uruguay,
    with no shared region, religion, or colonial ruler — point to whatever survives the variation.
    The two designs answer different causal questions: MSSD hunts factors <em>sufficient</em> to
    produce the outcome in a given context; MDSD hunts factors <em>necessary</em> across contexts.
    Real research typically runs both directions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "comparing two countries proves nothing." A well-chosen pair
    can be decisive — the classic smallpox-vaccine era epidemiology ran on exactly this logic
    where experiments were impossible. The catch is <strong>case selection</strong>: comparisons
    are only as good as the choice of cases, and two rules govern it. First, select cases to
    <em>vary the outcome</em> — the notorious error of "selection on the dependent variable"
    (explaining revolutions by studying only revolutions) produces factors that are ubiquitous
    rather than causal; Geddes's critique dismantled a generation of revolution literature that
    never looked at the non-revolutions. Second, select on the regression line for quantitative
    pairing — cases typical of the relationship you mean to explain — while reserving
    <strong>deviant cases</strong> (outliers) for mechanism-hunting: what the model cannot
    explain is where its story lives or dies.
  </p>

  <h2>Case studies: the instrument, not the consolation</h2>
  <p>
    The case study earns its place three ways. <strong>Hypothesis generation:</strong> deep
    knowledge of one case surfaces variables no dataset codes (the politics of land tenure inside
    a peasant village; the informal rules of a party machine). <strong>Mechanism testing:</strong>
    regression says X predicts Y; only inside a case can you watch X produce Y — which is where
    <strong>process tracing</strong> operates. Its modern, formalised version (Bennett and
    Checkel) treats each piece of case evidence as a Bayesian update: for each hypothesis, ask how
    likely the evidence is if the hypothesis is true versus if it is false. <strong>Hoop tests</strong>
    (evidence the theory says must exist — if the parliament never voted, the "legislation
    mattered" story dies for that case) eliminate; <strong>smoking-gun tests</strong> (evidence
    only that theory predicts — a secretly recorded quid pro quo) confirm; and the sequence of
    such tests, each specified in advance, turns a single case into a genuine inference engine.
    <strong>Crucial-case logic:</strong> an easy case that the theory fails (or a least-likely case
    it passes) carries unusual weight — the least-likely design is why single-case work on
    surprising outcomes (a democracy surviving in a poor, oil-dependent state) can overturn a
    generalisation.
  </p>

  <h2>Skocpol, Moore, and the classics as design exemplars</h2>
  <p>
    The field's canonical works double as methods textbooks. Skocpol's <em>States and Social
    Revolutions</em> ran a most-similar analysis across France 1789, Russia 1917 and China 1949:
    misery alone does not cause revolution (misery is ubiquitous), but <em>state crises</em> —
    fiscal-military breakdown under international pressure, combined with elite splits and
    organised peasant communities — appear in every case; her contrarian, structure-first
    conclusion ("revolutions come, they are not made") was built by paired comparison and became
    the template. Moore's <em>Social Origins of Dictatorship and Democracy</em> ("no bourgeoisie,
    no democracy") varied the landed-elite/merchant-bourgeois balance across eight countries and
    linked it to which political route each took — a typological comparison producing a theory of
    <em>routes</em> rather than a single cause. Modernisation theory (development brings democracy)
    survives in refined form as a survival law rather than a creation law: Przeworski and Limongi's
    analysis of every regime since 1950 found that above roughly a per-capita income threshold,
    <em>no</em> democracy has fallen — democracies are made by contingency and preserved by
    development, a finding whose derivation is a textbook paired-comparison argument.
  </p>

  <h2>The n = 200 problem and the mixed-methods settlement</h2>
  <p>
    Every design above strains against two hard constraints. <strong>Small n:</strong> ~200
    countries (fewer that are comparable, fewer still with data) cannot support large-N inference
    on many variables — the degrees of freedom collapse, and every added control eats a case.
    <strong>Non-independence:</strong> countries are not independent draws — diffusion (protest
    waves, constitutional borrowing), shared colonial origin, tied economies and global shocks
    correlate their fates; this is Galton's problem in modern dress, and it silently inflates the
    certainty of any regression that ignores it. The field's current settlement is
    <strong>mixed methods</strong>: large-N analysis to screen variables, establish scope and
    find the puzzles; small-N comparison and process tracing to establish mechanisms and
    adjudicate among competing stories; sometimes QCA (qualitative comparative analysis) as the
    bridge — set-theoretic logic over a dozen cases, testing which <em>combinations</em> of
    conditions (conjunctural causation) produce the outcome, because most political causation is
    "X matters <em>only with</em> Y", which neither regression's average effects nor Mill's single
    factor captures cleanly.
  </p>

  <h2>QCA: set-theoretic comparison in practice</h2>
  <p>
    Qualitative comparative analysis formalises the small-N craft. Cases are described by
    conditions (present or absent — crisp set; degree of membership 0 to 1 — fuzzy set), the
    outcome likewise, and the analysis seeks <strong>conjunctural causation</strong>: which
    combinations of conditions are sufficient for the outcome, and which conditions are
    necessary across all cases. The truth table for, say, twelve welfare-state cases over
    four conditions (strong left parties, Catholicism, affluence, union density) lists all
    combinations; Boolean minimisation reduces rows sharing implications into parsimonious
    formulas — yielding configurations like "left parties AND union density lead to
    comprehensive welfare" alongside the discovery that multiple different routes
    (<strong>equifinality</strong>) reach the same outcome. Its virtues: explicit
    sufficiency-and-necessity logic suited to policy configurations, and transparency — every
    case-to-row calibration is auditable. Its costs: limited cases force limited conditions
    (rows outrun cases fast), calibration is judgement-laden (the fuzzy memberships are the
    researcher's claims), and the conclusions are set-relations, not effect sizes — QCA
    answers "which recipe," not "how much does each ingredient add." The mature practice
    pairs it: QCA for configurations, regression for magnitudes, process tracing for
    mechanisms.
  </p>

  <h2>Historical institutionalism: path dependence and critical junctures</h2>
  <p>
    The comparative field's dominant historical framework explains why similar countries
    diverge and divergent ones converge — over time. <strong>Path dependence</strong>: early
    institutional choices create increasing returns (complementarities, sunk investments,
    constituencies built on the institution) that make switching costs rise with time — the
    QWERTY logic applied to polities: electoral systems persisting past their founding
    coalitions, health-care architectures locking in providers. <strong>Critical
    junctures</strong>: wars, depressions and crises open windows where structure is relaxed
    and agency weighs most; the paths locked in afterwards (the post-1945 settlements —
    welfare states, occupation-imposed constitutions — are the classical cases) explain
    decades of divergence from single formative moments. <strong>Layering, drift,
    conversion</strong> (Streeck and Thelen's taxonomy) describe gradual change: institutions
    transform by accretion, by erosion of their environment, or by repurposing to new ends —
    explaining why formal continuity coexists with substantive change (the same constitution
    governing different regimes). The framework's methodological payoff: it specifies
    <em>when</em> comparison is meaningful — within a path versus across paths — and it
    disciplines the case-selection logic of the classical designs.
  </p>

  <h2>Measurement in practice: democracy indices and their disputes</h2>
  <p>
    Comparative claims are only as good as their measures, and the democracy indices are the
    field's running measurement seminar. Polity coded authority regimes; Freedom House codes
    rights and liberties by expert judgement; V-Dem (Varieties of Democracy) disaggregates
    the concept — dozens of components (electoral, liberal, participatory, egalitarian,
    deliberative) each rated by hundreds of country experts, with a measurement model
    propagating uncertainty into the indices. The disputes are informative: different indices
    rank the same country differently (the disagreements are theory disagreements made
    visible — what counts as participation?), expert-rater noise is estimable (V-Dem's
    contribution), and thresholds change narratives (a country's "democratic backsliding"
    can begin in one index and not another — the definitional choice is the finding). The
    same logic extends to state capacity (tax extraction, bureaucratic quality) and conflict
    measures: every comparative inference inherits its variable's construction, which is why
    the mature methodological move is sensitivity analysis across measures — if the finding
    survives the index disagreement, it is probably about the world; if not, it was probably
    about the measure.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Natural experiments smuggled into the comparative method.</strong> Where history ran
    the randomisation — the Berlin Wall across Berlin, island vs mainland migration quotas, the
    arbitrary administrative borders drawn by colonial cartographers — regression-discontinuity
    and difference-in-differences designs extract experimental-grade causal answers from
    non-experimental settings, and this is now comparative politics' most credentialled causal
    genre (the institutional-origins literature, from Acemoglu-Robinson's settler-mortality
    instrument to border-discontinuity studies of ethnic favouritism, runs on it).
    <strong>Concept formation is measurement.</strong> The field's quiet methodological crisis is
    conceptual: "democracy", "state capacity" and "populism" must mean the same thing across 200
    cases to be compared, and Sartori's ladder of abstraction, along with modern efforts
    (V-Dem's disaggregated indices measured by hundreds of country experts, with measurement-error
    models propagated through the analysis) is the ongoing engineering response — a reminder that
    in comparative work, defining the variable <em>is</em> half the design.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> whether process tracing's Bayesian form can be standardised
    without mechanising judgement; how to aggregate evidence across heterogeneous designs
    (meta-analysis for case studies is methodologically unfinished); whether machine-learning
    text analysis of legislative and media corpora can industrialise process tracing; and how
    comparative method should handle polities whose outcomes increasingly travel together —
    democratic backsliding diffusing across regions raises the stakes on Galton's problem from
    technical embarrassment to first-order political question.
  </p>
  <p>
    One final design note: the comparative method's unit of observation is shifting.
    Subnational comparison — within-country regions, municipalities, states — multiplies
    cases while holding national context constant: the most-similar logic at a scale where
    more observations exist (the German Länder, US states and Indian states as comparative
    laboratories). Time-series cross-section panels (countries observed over decades) merge
    the comparative and statistical traditions, with unit effects, period effects and the
    Galton problem modelled rather than ignored. The classical designs remain the
    intellectual core — every panel model is Mill's logic wearing matrices — but the
    observation base is expanding, and with it the questions the method can reach: policy
    diffusion, federal dynamics, and the within-country variation that national aggregates
    average away.
  </p>

